abstract: >-
  Data augmentation is routinely prescribed for the class imbalance of network intrusion detection datasets,
  and studies report gains from oversampling and generative models. We examine whether these gains survive a
  leakage-aware evaluation and what explains the outcome. Five flow-based datasets from two feature families
  are evaluated under four splitting protocols with six classifier families, nine published augmentation
  methods and a constraint-aware diffusion augmenter built as a probe. The evaluation protocol outweighs the
  choice of method: 27–66% of the flows are exact duplicates once identifiers are removed, and a temporal or
  host-disjoint split lowers macro-F1 by 7.1 and 10.3 points on average relative to a deduplicated random
  split. With the complete training data, in which the largest classes are capped, no method improves macro-F1
  significantly and nine of ten lower the Matthews correlation coefficient. When rare classes are reduced to
  1–5% of their size, synthetic minority oversampling (SMOTE) gains four to five points, but class weights
  achieve the same without generating any flow; without class capping, oversampling shows a non-significant
  gain of about two points. Between 20% and 99% of the synthetic flows of published interpolation and
  generative methods violate protocol-level constraints that real flows almost always satisfy. The probe
  removes all violations and outperforms the other augmenters, yet is not significantly better than no
  augmentation (+0.008 macro-F1, p = 0.20), and its validation-based decisions do not transfer to the test
  partition. Under sound protocols scores follow class overlap and train–test shift, which resampling does not
  change. Code, splits and results are released.
